  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of what Hipparchus invented and discovered:\n\n---\n\n## Hipparchus \u2013 Inventions & Discoveries\n\nHipparchus is often referred to as the **\"father of scientific astronomy\"** and is usually regarded as the greatest astronomer of ancient times. He was born in Nicaea, Bithynia (now Iznik, Turkey), circa 190 B.C.\n\nHere are his major contributions:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd3a Trigonometry\nTrigonometry was probably **invented by Hipparchus**, who compiled a table of the chords of angles and made them available to other scholars. He developed trigonometry and constructed trigonometric tables, and he solved several problems of spherical trigonometry.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u2b50 First Star Catalog\nUsing a thin tube called a **dioptra** to scan the heavens, Hipparchus made extensive observations of star positions and produced the **first-known catalog of stars**. He is credited with creating the first reliable star catalog, which included approximately **850 stars** and established a system of stellar magnitude.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83c\udf00 Precession of the Equinoxes\nHipparchus discovered the **precession of the equinoxes** by comparing his notes with earlier observers; his realization that the points of solstice and equinox moved slowly from east to west against the fixed background of the stars was one of his most significant achievements.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udd2d The Astrolabe\nHipparchus is credited with the **invention or improvement of several astronomical instruments**. According to Synesius of Ptolemais, he made the first *astrolabion*, which may have been an armillary sphere or the predecessor of the planar instrument called the **astrolabe**. With an astrolabe, Hipparchus was the first to be able to measure the geographical latitude and time by observing stars.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83c\udf0d Latitude & Longitude / Geographic Coordinates\nHe developed tools for measuring celestial angles and contributed to the **systematic use of geographic coordinates**, pioneering the application of **latitude and longitude**.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83c\udf11 Predicting Solar Eclipses\nBased on his solar and lunar theories and his numerical trigonometry, he was probably the **first to develop a reliable method to predict solar eclipses**.\n\n---\n\n### 7. \ud83c\udf19 Earth-Moon Distance\nHe **measured the Earth-Moon distance accurately**, founded the mathematical discipline of trigonometry, and his combinatorics work was unequalled until 1870.\n\n---\n\n### 8. \ud83d\udcbb Possibly the Antikythera Mechanism\nHe is one of the strongest contenders for the honor of being the **inventor of the Antikythera Mechanism** (also known as the Antikythera Device), considered the **world's first analogue computer**.\n\n---\n\n### 9. \ud83d\udcd0 The 360-Degree Circle System\nHipparchus adopted the Babylonian system of **dividing a circle into 360 degrees** and dividing each degree into 60 arc minutes \u2014 a system so convenient that we still use it today!\n\n---\n\n### 10. \u2600\ufe0f Models of the Sun and Moon\nHe was the **first whose quantitative and accurate models for the motion of the Sun and Moon** survive.\n\n---\n\nDespite his geocentric views, which posited Earth at the center of the universe, Hipparchus's work laid important groundwork for future astronomers, including Ptolemy, whose own writings would dominate the field until the Renaissance.",
